        <comment>Steinsaltz reports that some Ashkenazim begin at Joel 2:11 and most at 2:15, without saying which communities add 2:11-14, so this is recorded rather than assigned.</comment>
      </variant>
      <comment>Torah Temimah prints Hosea, Joel and Micah together without saying which custom reads which, so it does not settle this; hamichlol and Torat Chaim both give Ashkenaz Joel 2:15. Steinsaltz adds that some Ashkenazim read both the Joel and the Micah passages.</comment>
    </custom>
    <custom n="Sefard, Romania" sources="michlol">
      <span book="Hosea" from="14:2" to="14:10"/>
      <span book="Micah" from="7:18" to="7:20"/>
    </custom>
    <custom n="Italki, Poznan" book="Isaiah">
      <span from="55:6" to="56:8"/>
    </custom>
    <custom n="Teiman" sources="torat-chaim" book="Hosea">
      <span from="14:2" to="14:10"/>
    </custom>
    <custom n="Chabad" reads="inherit" sources="chitas">
      <comment>Chabad reads with Sefard here, verified in Chitas. hamichlol names no Chabad practice at this week, and its rite key nests Chabad under Ashkenaz, so it would imply the Ashkenaz reading above; Chitas is against that.</comment>
    </custom>
    <custom n="Romania" reads="inherit">
      <comment>hamichlol gives Romania the same reading as Sefard here.</comment>
    </custom>
    <custom n="Poznan" reads="inherit">
      <comment>Poznan per the first practice in kuntres Poznan, which reads Dirshu on Shabbos Shuvah; its second practice reads Shuva like the rest of Ashkenaz. hamichlol documents the Poznan readings from the community pinkas (poznan-pinkas).</comment>
    </custom>
  </week>
